+- [Should-fix] §4.1 revision line / §4.4 header "Your instructions follow (graph revision <rev>); follow them"; §2.4 "takes effect at each affected seat's next /seat"; §4.5 "already in context if the session-start hook injected the same revision" — Instructions are re-injected into contexts that can still hold an earlier render (SessionStart on resume, repeated `/seat`), and the spec gives the model no rule that the newest render replaces earlier ones. The only conflict rule, "on conflict the later section wins", orders sections within one render. After `plan instructions clear` or a narrowing edit, the old text stays in force with nothing withdrawing it. (new)
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: All three seats fetched https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks: "`SessionStart` hooks run again on resume with `source` set to `\"resume\"` … so they can refresh their context" and "Claude Code saves the injected text in the session transcript". Spec grep for replace, supersede, earlier, stale, resume, compact finds no supersession rule; §4.5 uses the revision only to skip re-reading. §2.4's promise that the edit "takes effect at each affected seat's next /seat (start, clear, compaction)" is not guaranteed for clears and narrowings on resume or live `/seat`. Not Blocking: compaction and clear drop the old context and a model will usually prefer the newer revision.
+  fix-shape hint: Add one header sentence ("this replaces any earlier herdr-graph instruction render in this conversation") and soften the §2.4 warning to say a cleared instruction may linger in a resumed or live session until context is reset.
